<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Retail partnership priorities"><div><strong>Product fit</strong><span>Customers should have a real reason to choose a science-informed hydration mix in that channel.</span></div><div><strong>Education fit</strong><span>Retail messaging should explain the product accurately rather than depend on exaggerated hydration claims.</span></div><div><strong>Operational fit</strong><span>Assortment, forecasting, replenishment, merchandising and communication should be workable for both sides.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Retail principle</p><h2>Distribution is useful only when the product belongs on the shelf</h2><p>Best Hydrate is a Canadian hydration powder with a science-led positioning that spans performance, everyday and future evidence-building applications. That does not mean every retailer or distributor is automatically a fit. The strongest channels are those where customers already care about hydration, performance, wellness, practical daily use or evidence-informed products.</p><p>We would rather grow through the right accounts than create broad placement that depends on confusing or unsupported claims.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/1366594/pexels-photo-1366594.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Fully stocked supermarket shelves viewed from above" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where we may fit</p><h2>Specialty, health, performance and selected mainstream channels</h2><p>Potential partners can include independent retailers, supplement and performance stores, health-oriented shops, regional chains, distributors and other channels where hydration education and repeat purchase make sense.</p><p>Channel fit should be evaluated using the actual customer, shelf environment, competing products, merchandising requirements and operational capacity rather than brand ambition alone.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">What a retail conversation should cover</p><h2>Four practical questions</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Who buys it?</h3><p>Define the customer need and why the product adds something useful to the assortment.</p></article><article><h3>How is it explained?</h3><p>Align product education, staff talking points and shelf copy with the authorized product and current evidence.</p></article><article><h3>How is it supplied?</h3><p>Set realistic expectations for ordering, lead times, replenishment, case configuration and communication.</p></article><article><h3>How is success measured?</h3><p>Review sell-through, repeat purchase, customer feedback and whether the channel remains a good fit.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">What we can provide</p><h2>Product context that helps partners sell without overselling</h2><p>The rebuilt site gives retail partners a clearer education layer than a package alone can provide: Product Details, Directions, Hydration Science, Formula rationale and situation-specific Solutions pages. Those resources can support staff education and customer questions while keeping regulated and research-sensitive claims in the right place.</p><p>Commercial terms, logistics and assortment decisions should be discussed directly rather than published as universal terms on the public site.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Commercial standard</span><h3>Good sell-through should come from fit, not inflated promises.</h3><p>Credible education is part of the channel strategy.</p></aside></section>

